Adam Newman, M.D. wrote:

> Can't figure out how to try out wizard mode on Mac OSX! Tried the command
> line option in the terminal, but that doesn't cause the app to run.
> Obviously no way to do that from the GUI...

* Create a Mac OS X account having the short name 'wizard'. That
account does not have to be an administrative user, and indeed probably
shouldn't be.

* Log in as 'wizard'.

* Run 'Terminal'.

* Assuming you have installed 'NetHack.app' in '/Applications', and
have not moved your playground ('nethackdir') out of the 'NetHack.app'
package, run the following as one command in 'Terminal':

    /Applications/NetHack/NetHack.app/Contents/MacOS/nethack
    -d /Applications/NetHack/NetHack.app/nethackdir -D

Because you are specifying both the program and playground locations,
you don't need to change the current directory before running this
command.

* I think coupling the NetHack playground to the NetHack application
package is poor design. I also like to keep my wizard-mode playground
separate from my normal playground, mostly to prevent my accidentally
uploading wizard-mode bones to Hearse. For those reasons, I have moved
my playgrounds outside the NetHack package. If you choose to do this,
change the above command to something like

    /Applications/NetHack/NetHack.app/Contents/MacOS/nethack
    -d ~/NetHack/playground -D

where (in this example) '~/NetHack/playground' is the out-of-package
playground location for the currently logged-on user (i.e., what was
'nethackdir' in the program bundle). If you run this exact command for
both 'wizard' and non-'wizard' users, then each user will have a
private playground separate from other users and outside the program
package.

* If you want to run wizard mode with a predefined wizard kit, you need
to set the *exported* environment variable 'WIZKIT' to the path of your
wizard-kit text file. You must set 'WIZKIT' before running 'nethack'.

